\section{Diagnostic fragment matching}{

Fragment presence is evaluated using the peak m/z values in
\code{denoised_data$denoised_ms_data}, after denoising. Failed spectra
are excluded. The original MS2 peak arrays in \code{ms_data_raw} are
not used for fragment detection.

For an observed peak m/z value \code{observed} and a target fragment
m/z value \code{target}, a match satisfies:
\itemize{
\item Relative tolerance:
\code{abs((observed - target) / target) * 1e6 <= mass_error_tolerance_val}.
\item Absolute tolerance:
\code{abs(observed - target) <= mass_error_tolerance_val}.
}

A fragment is present if at least one peak matches its target.
Only fragments referenced in \code{diagnostic_frags_exp} are evaluated.
Including additional fragments in \code{diagnostic_frags_list} does
not require them to be present.

Each fragment name in the expression represents a logical presence
value. For example, \code{"HexNAc & HexNAc_ProA"} requires both fragments,
\code{"HexNAc | Hex"} requires either fragment, and
\code{"HexNAc & !Bisecting"} requires HexNAc without a detected
Bisecting fragment.

No additional intensity cutoff or fragment charge correction is
applied. Target values are used directly as m/z values.
Fragment tests are independent: the same observed peak can satisfy
multiple targets when their tolerance windows overlap.
Supply finite peak m/z values; missing values are not explicitly
removed before presence evaluation.
}

\section{Association with MS1 spectra}{

For each matching MS2 spectrum, the function selects the MS1
spectrum in \code{ms_data_raw} with the smallest positive retention-time
difference from that MS2 spectrum.

MS1 spectra at the same retention time as the MS2 spectrum are
excluded. If several MS1 spectra share the closest earlier time,
the first in the input MS1 sequence is selected. No maximum
retention-time gap is imposed.

Association uses retention time only; it does not inspect an
explicit precursor-scan link. Retention times in both objects
must use the same units. If a matching MS2 spectrum has no earlier
MS1 spectrum, the function stops with an error.
}

\section{Diagnostic fragment signal plot}{

The signal plot is built from the matching spectra only. Its fragment
set is every fragment referenced in \code{diagnostic_frags_exp}, labelled
as a positive condition (not negated) or a negative condition
(negated by \code{!}, including through enclosing parentheses).
Matching spectra are selected using the positive part of the
expression, with negated terms removed.

For each matching spectrum and each fragment, the peak with the
highest intensity within the mass-error tolerance window is
selected. Fragments with no peak in the window (for example, negative
conditions) have no matched m/z, no mass error, and are not drawn.

The plot has two panels sharing the fragment m/z axis:
\itemize{
\item Top: min-max normalized intensity of the matched peaks,
with a line connecting the fragments of each spectrum.
\item Bottom: signed mass error of the matched peaks, in the
unit given by \code{mass_error_tolerance_type}, with horizontal
jitter to reduce overplotting.
}
Points are coloured by positive or negative condition. Intensities
are normalized across all plotted points, not within each spectrum.
}

